@inject IDeliveryService Places
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer
@inherits CircuitScopedComponent

@* FR-104's place picker: one address box, its answers, and the map it fills.

   Written four times before this - a pickup and a dropoff on the dispatch board, a pickup and a
   dropoff on the client's request dialog - which is how the two dialogs drifted apart in the first
   place, when the side-by-side layout was applied to one pair and not the other. The column is the
   same column on every screen; only the stem in its ids, the map's label and the point it fills
   differ, so those are parameters and nothing else is.

   The stem is interpolated rather than spelled out because the hooks the suite pins - `pickup-search`,
   `dt-pickup-search`, `dt-pickup-empty`, `dt-pickup-match` and their dropoff and request-* twins -
   are how a test tells one box from another, and four literals per copy is four places to paste the
   wrong one.

   Every handler is built in C# below rather than written as a lambda in an attribute: a `=>` here
   would close the tag as far as NFR-14's markup scan is concerned, and everything after it would be
   read as rendered English.

   The row and the two columns stay on the screens. A picker knows what it renders; how two of them
   are laid out beside each other is the dialog's question, and a component that answered it would
   have to be told which half of the row it is. *@

@* No `for` on the map's label below: a map is a region rather than a labelable control, and a `for`
   pointing at one would be an association a browser ignores. DtMap carries its own aria-label. The
   search box here is an input and has one. *@
<label class="form-label dt-type-label" for="@BoxId">@Localizer["AddressSearch"]</label>
<div class="input-group">
    <input id="@BoxId" class="form-control"
           @bind="Search.Query"
           @bind:event="oninput"
           @onkeydown="Enter" />
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary @SearchHook"
            disabled="@Search.IsBusy"
            @onclick="Run">
        <Icon Name="IconName.Search" />@Localizer["AddressSearchAction"]
    </button>
</div>
<div class="form-text">@Localizer["AddressSearchHint"]</div>
@* The box's own banner, under the box it is about (DW-41). The dialog's banner above belongs to
   whatever the form submits, and a search that writes into it is a search that can erase a refusal
   nobody has read. *@
<DtFailureBanner Failures="Search.Failures" />
@if (Search.FoundNothing)
{
    @* role="status", like the timeline's empty line: "the search found nothing" is the one outcome
       with no visible change to notice, and a user who cannot see the box is otherwise told nothing
       at all (NFR-22). *@
    <div class="form-text @EmptyHook" role="status">@Localizer["AddressSearchEmpty"]</div>
}
@if (Search.Matches.Count > 0)
{
    <ul class="list-group mb-2 dt-addr-matches">
        @foreach (var match in Search.Matches)
        {
            <li class="list-group-item">
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-link @MatchHook"
                        @onclick="Pick(match)">@match.Address</button>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}
<label class="form-label dt-type-label">@Label</label>
@* The search row above is a sibling of <DtMap>, never a child of it: Leaflet builds its own DOM
   inside that container, outside Blazor's diff, so anything rendered inside would be wiped on the
   first pan. It is the structural rule this file exists to keep - the box and the map are two
   elements beside each other, and nesting them reads as tidier right up to the first drag. *@
<DtMap Value="Value" ValueChanged="Picked" Picker="true" />
@* Under the map rather than in the dialog's banner: an unpicked point is refused with
   COMMON_FIELD_REQUIRED - "Обов’язкове поле" - which says nothing at all about which control, and
   the map is half a screen tall. Beneath it, the sentence needs nothing more. *@
<DtFieldError Field="@Field" Failures="Failures" />

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The stem every hook on this column is built from — <c>pickup</c>, <c>dropoff</c>,
    /// <c>request-pickup</c>, <c>request-dropoff</c>. It is the only thing that tells two pickers on
    /// one screen apart, in the markup and in the suite alike.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Prefix { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// The map's label, already localized. The search box above it is labelled from this
    /// component's own resources, because "address search" is the same words above every map; what
    /// differs is the point, and only the caller knows whether this one is a pickup or a dropoff.
    /// <para>
    /// Required, because its absence says nothing: the map simply loses its caption, and a column
    /// with no caption still looks like a column.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string? Label { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The CLR property name a validator would put on a refusal about this point — <c>Pickup</c> or
    /// <c>Dropoff</c> — so <see cref="DtFieldError" /> can claim it from the screen's sink.
    /// <para>
    /// Required, and it is the quietest of the four to leave out: the control below the map then
    /// claims nothing, so an unpicked point is refused with a sentence that appears nowhere at all -
    /// on a page that otherwise renders exactly as it should.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string? Field { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The box this column renders, owned by the caller's form rather than built here.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Deliberately the caller's: a form constructs its pair beside the two fields they fill, which
    /// is what keeps "the dropoff box sets the dropoff" one readable line - and a static-rendering
    /// harness can drive that line directly. State built in here would put the one invariant that is
    /// easy to cross and invisible in a diff somewhere no test can reach.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public PlaceSearch Search { get; set; } = new(_ => { });

    /// <summary>The point this picker fills. Bound to the caller's form field.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MapLocation? Value { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised whenever the point moves, by a map click or by a chosen match.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Raised for a chosen match as well as for a map click, and that is not belt and braces. The
    /// box's own assignment writes the caller's field, but an event handled inside this component
    /// re-renders only this component - so without telling the parent, the caller's <c>Value</c>
    /// would still be the old point on the next pass and the map would sit where it was while the
    /// address list said otherwise.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<MapLocation> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The screen's own refusal sink, for the message under the map. Not this box's search failures,
    /// which are its own and are rendered above (DW-41).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<ScreenFailure> Failures { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The search input's id, and what its label points at.</summary>
    private string BoxId => $"{Prefix}-search";

    /// <summary>The hook on the search button.</summary>
    private string SearchHook => $"dt-{Prefix}-search";

    /// <summary>The hook on the found-nothing line.</summary>
    private string EmptyHook => $"dt-{Prefix}-empty";

    /// <summary>The hook on every match in the list.</summary>
    private string MatchHook => $"dt-{Prefix}-match";

    /// <summary>
    /// The search action.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Nothing is assigned here, and that is DW-41: the box keeps its own failures, so a search that
    /// succeeds cannot clear a save refusal the user has not read - nor the other box's. The
    /// three-character floor is the capability's, not this column's, and is deliberately not
    /// repeated: a rule stated twice is a rule that can disagree with itself, and the hint under the
    /// box is what says so beforehand.
    /// </remarks>
    private Task Run() => Search.RunAsync(Places.SearchPlacesAsync, CircuitToken);

    /// <summary>
    /// The same action on Enter, which is the gesture a search box invites. Anything else typed in
    /// the box falls through and is ignored.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// No <c>preventDefault</c> to go with it, and none is needed: every caller renders this outside
    /// its <c>EditForm</c> precisely so Enter has no form to submit.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The busy check is the same guard the button carries as <c>disabled</c>. Enter is a key that
    /// can be held down, and without it an impatient second press during a lookup is a second
    /// request to a public service whose slower answer then overwrites the faster one's matches.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task Enter(KeyboardEventArgs args)
    {
        if (args.Key == "Enter" && !Search.IsBusy)
        {
            await Run();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A match's selection action, bound as a delegate for the reason the others are.</summary>
    private Func<Task> Pick(PlaceMatch match) => () => ChooseAsync(match);

    /// <summary>Applies a match: the box moves the point, and the caller is told it moved.</summary>
    private Task ChooseAsync(PlaceMatch match)
    {
        Search.Choose(match);

        return ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(match.Point);
    }

    /// <summary>A point picked on the map itself, passed straight up.</summary>
    private Task Picked(MapLocation point) => ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(point);
}
